This PR supports simplest newtype wrapping for callee path if the following conditions are met:
- Signature function is in trait,
- `reuse` is in trait impl of a newtype (struct with a constructor that has one field),
- Delegation path resolution equals signature function,
- The return type of the signature function is `Self`.

Example:
```rust
trait Trait {
    fn method(&self) -> Self;
    fn r#static() -> Self;
    fn raw_S(&self) -> S { S }
}

struct S;
impl Trait for S {
    fn method(&self) -> S { S }
    fn r#static() -> S { S }
}

struct W(S);
impl Trait for W {
    reuse Trait::*{ self.0 }

    // Desugaring:
    #[attr = Inline(Hint)]
    fn method(self: _) -> _ { W(Trait::method(self.0)) }
    #[attr = Inline(Hint)]
    fn r#static() -> _ { W(Trait::r#static()) }
    #[attr = Inline(Hint)]
    fn raw_S(self: _) -> _ { Trait::raw_S(self.0) }
}
```

If the above conditions are met, there is no need to propagate generics of a newtype, as unused generics is an error, thus they should be used in a single field and it can be inferred from the return type of callee path.

Accessing signatures through queries produced query cycles in one test, so `with_no_trimmed_paths` was used to prevent it, though it can cause other cycles in other situations maybe.

Part of rust-lang/rust#118212.
